# 33. Packages and extensions

A program names modules and nothing else. This chapter follows one
`import` from the module it names down to the file on disk that runs, and
names each unit on the way. [[05-modules/packages|Modules and packages]]
introduced the four terms; here they are put to work on a package that is
not part of `hql`.

## Four units, one path

![An import resolves to a module, the module to the package that provides it, and the package to its contract and its extension, which is compiled into hql for a builtin package and loaded from a library for an installed one.](33-resolution.svg)

| Unit | What it is | For `import hello` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| module | a named file of declarations; what `import` names | `hello` |
| package | what is installed: a module root and the extension implementing it | `hello` |
| extension | the Rust functions behind a package's declarations | the `hello` extension |
| library | the compiled file an installed extension is loaded from | `libhql_ext_hello.dylib` |

The package of this chapter is `hello`, a small package kept in the `hql`
repository under
[`tests/packages/hello/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/tests/packages/hello).
[[34-writing-an-extension|Chapter 34]] writes it from an empty directory.

## An import names a module

```hql
import hello

hello.greet("Ada")
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: no module named `hello` under examples/tutorial/lib
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | import hello
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

In the tutorial vault nothing provides `hello`: no package does, and the
vault's module root, `examples/tutorial/lib`, holds no such file. The
import is refused.
The program is the same in a workspace where a package does provide it:

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

hello.greet("Ada")
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

Neither program names a package. Which package provides `hello`, and
whether one does, is a fact about the environment the program runs in.

## A module has one provider

When `hql` starts it gathers the packages of the environment: the two
builtin ones, the packages installed under the HQL home, and the crates a
workspace lists ([[36-installing-packages|chapter 36]]). Each package
states the modules it provides. A module has exactly one provider. Two
packages that provide one module are both refused: the order in which
packages are found picks no winner.

`hql builtins` names the provider beside each module:

```sh
hql --workspace examples/packages builtins
```

```text
math (std 0.7.0, prelude)
…
hello (hello 0.1.0, imported where it is used)
  greet
    String | greet() -> String
    pure, barrier
```

`math` comes from the package `std` at the version of `hql` itself.
`hello` comes from the package `hello` at the version of its own crate.

## A package is a contract and an extension

A package holds two things, as `std` does
([[32-functions-and-declarations/how-std-is-built|chapter 32]]):

- a **module root**, a directory of contracts, one per module it provides;
- an **extension**, one Rust function per name those contracts declare.

For a builtin package both are inside the `hql` binary. For an installed
package the module root is a directory of `.hql` files and the extension
is a library beside it:

```text
~/.hql/lib/hello/
├── package.toml              what was built, from what, against which hql
├── modules/
│   └── hello/mod.hql         the contract of module `hello`
└── libhql_ext_hello.dylib    the extension, as a library
```

The library is `.dylib` on macOS, `.so` on Linux and `.dll` on Windows.

## What an import does

`import hello` makes the declarations of the module `hello` visible to the
program, under the name `hello`. By the time a program is checked the
package is already bound: its contract was read, its library was loaded,
and each declaration was paired with its function. The import is a matter
of names. That is why a call to a function of a package is checked as any
call is:

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

hello.greet(3)
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `greet` needs String, not Int
 --> <expression>:3:1
  |
3 | hello.greet(3)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

The refusal comes from the contract, `fn greet(name: String) : String`.
The Rust function is never called with an `Int`.

A name of a package used without its import is reported as a missing
import, since the environment knows which module declares it:

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
"hi".shout()
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `shout` is declared in the module `hello`: write `import hello`
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | "hi".shout()
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

## What is next

[[34-writing-an-extension|Chapter 34]] writes the `hello` package: its
crate, its contract, and the Rust functions behind it.
